course: dictionary
lesson: 16048
title: Words for watching and waiting
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A vigil is a time when you stay awake to watch over someone or something, often at night. It is a noun, so it names a thing: a vigil. «Mum kept a vigil by her bed all night while the baby was poorly.» Mum did not sleep. She stayed awake to watch over the baby, so vigil means a night of watching.
explain: Vigil can also mean a quiet gathering. «After the flood, the village held a candlelit vigil for the people who had lost their homes.» People came together, often with candles, to remember someone or to show they care. Look for clues like candles, quiet, or remember in the words around it.
explain: Churches use the word too. «On Holy Saturday night, the church held the Easter Vigil.» Here a vigil is a service on the evening before a holy day. In The Wind in the Willows, Rat and Mole keep watch through the night, looking for little Portly. Their watching is a vigil too.
fact: A vigil is a time when you stay awake, especially at night, to watch over someone or something, or to pray.
fact: «Vigil» can mean a quiet gathering of people, often with candles, to remember someone or show they care.
fact: In church, a vigil is a service on the evening before a holy day, such as the Easter Vigil.
fact: Context clues for «vigil» include words like night, candles, watch over, and remember.
fact: A good check: put «a time of staying awake to watch» in place of the word and see if the sentence still makes sense.
exercise: «Mum kept a vigil by her bed all night.» What does vigil mean here? | A loud argument | *A time of staying awake to watch over someone | A bedtime story | A long sleep in a comfy bed
exercise: «The village held a candlelit vigil for the fisherman.» Why did they light candles? | *To remember him and show they cared | To cook their dinner | To give light for a party | To warm the church up
exercise: «On Holy Saturday night, the church held the Easter Vigil.» In this sentence, what kind of event is a vigil? | A sleepover with friends | A football match | A school trip to the zoo | *A church service on the evening before a holy day
test: «Rat and Mole kept vigil all night, looking for little Portly.» What does kept vigil mean? | Went to bed early | Sang songs until morning | Ran home quickly | *Stayed awake to watch and look for him
test: «Many people joined the vigil with candles.» What is a vigil in this sentence? | A kind of breakfast | A race around the park | *A quiet gathering to remember someone | A noisy party
exam: Ayesha says a vigil is just a party. Why is she wrong? | Vigils always happen in the daytime | Vigils only happen at birthdays | *A vigil is about staying awake to watch or remembering someone, not a party | A vigil is a party with candles, so she is right
